UPDATE Beats SET [Text]=N'Teo hadn''t been inside Household 12 since the load survey, and the room hadn''t changed in the months since, except that she noticed different things this time, coming to it with a question instead of a tape measure. Books on every wall, floor to ceiling in places, the spines faded unevenly where forty years of light had reached some shelves harder than others. Photographs in frames on every flat surface that wasn''t already given over to books, none of them recent, all of them arranged with a care that looked less like decoration and more like a kind of filing system only Dagny herself could read. A bed against the far wall that hadn''t moved, Teo would have bet, since the day it was carried in.

Dagny let her in without asking why she''d come, which told Teo, before either of them said anything, that the older woman already knew.

"You''ve seen the sheet," Teo said.

"Everyone''s seen the sheet," Dagny said. "You made sure of that. It''s a good sheet. Accurate, as far as I can tell, though I haven''t checked your arithmetic and don''t intend to." She didn''t invite Teo to sit, and didn''t sit herself, and the two of them stood in the narrow space between the books and the bed the way two people stand when neither has decided whether the conversation will be short.

"I''m not here to ask how you''re going to vote," Teo said. "I want to be clear about that before I say anything else, because I think you''d know if I were lying about it, and I don''t want to start this by lying to you."

"Then what are you here for?"

"I ran your household''s allocation. I posted it. I watched Priya read it this morning and do the arithmetic herself, standing at the wall, the way I did it at my desk last night. I think you should know what that number does to other people before you decide anything, not because I want to move you, but because I don''t think it''s honest to let you make a choice without knowing the shape it lands in."

Dagny looked at her for a long moment, not unkindly, the way she''d looked at the room at every meeting Teo had watched her sit through — a stillness that gave away nothing, that Teo had learned by now wasn''t agreement and wasn''t disagreement, just a woman declining to spend any part of herself on being read.

"I know the shape it lands in," Dagny said. "I''ve known it since before you ran it. I''ve lived in this bloc longer than you''ve been alive, and I''ve watched people do arithmetic with my life in it before, and I want to tell you something plainly, since you''ve come here to be plain with me. I don''t want the question. Not the one you''re not asking and not the one everyone thinks you are. I don''t want to sit in this room and be asked to weigh forty years of my own walls against a twelve-year-old''s shortfall, because there isn''t a version of that weighing that leaves me whole on either side of it, and I refuse to spend what''s left of my life performing the weighing for an audience that''s already decided what answer would make them feel better about the vote."

"I''m not asking you to perform anything," Teo said. "I came here because I thought you deserved to hear the number from me instead of secondhand."

"I appreciate that," Dagny said, and Teo believed her. "I''m not angry at you for the sheet. You did your job the way you always do it, plainly, without dressing it up for anyone''s comfort, and I respect that more than you probably know. I''m telling you that I''m not going to let the sheet turn into a conversation I have to keep having, in this room, with every person in this bloc who decides they have a right to know what I''m going to do with something that''s mine."

"Nobody''s asking you to hand anything over," Teo said. "Nobody''s dying if you don''t. I want that said out loud, because I think somewhere in this bloc people have started talking about your allocation like it''s a choice between your household and Priya''s daughter, and it isn''t that. It''s a vote, same as anyone''s, and a number, same as anyone''s, and you get to cast the first without explaining the second to a single soul in Candelaria if you don''t want to."

Something in Dagny''s face shifted at that, not softened exactly, but loosened, the way a held breath loosens when someone finally says the thing a person has been waiting to hear without knowing they were waiting.

"That''s the first true thing anyone''s said to me about this in weeks," she said. "Everyone else has come in here already certain of what my number means. You''re the first to walk in and tell me it doesn''t have to mean anything at all beyond what it is."

"It doesn''t," Teo said. "Not to me. I ran a number. I posted a number. What you do with your vote is yours, and I''m not going to pretend I know what that number should cost you, because I''ve learned this year that I don''t know what any number costs anyone until they tell me, and you haven''t told me anything, and you don''t owe me that either."

Dagny didn''t answer right away. She looked at the books instead, at the photographs, at forty years arranged the way only she could read them, and when she finally spoke again, her voice had lost none of its stillness.

"I want the question to not exist," she said. "That''s the whole of my answer, if you''re looking for one. I''m not going to give you more than that, not because I''m hiding anything, but because there isn''t more than that to give. I''ll vote when the vote comes. I won''t explain it before, and I won''t explain it after, and I''d ask the same of you that I asked of the room three weeks ago — let that be enough, because it''s the only thing I have that''s actually mine to give."

Teo left without pressing further, because there was nothing left in the room to press, no object offered, no object taken, nobody in that narrow space between the books and the bed in any danger at all — only a number on a wall and a woman who''d decided, plainly and without cruelty, that some questions didn''t deserve an answer just because the whole bloc wanted one.' WHERE Id='019F3AC8-397D-76D9-B315-C96E26515F40';